## Agreed behaviour and implemented features

Enter a term's start/end dates and timezone, courses, and one week's sessions. Generate weekly occurrences through the inclusive term end. A weekly session specifies title, course, weekday, start/end times, and tags. The current app supports same-day sessions; overnight sessions must be split.

Each occurrence is independently marked done or undone. Completion keys are `<lecture-id>:<YYYY-MM-DD>`; completion values are timestamps. Marking one week's occurrence complete does not complete every week.

- **Upcoming:** the scheduled end has not yet been reached, and the occurrence is not explicitly complete. An ongoing lecture therefore remains Upcoming.
- **Unfinished:** the device time has reached/passed its scheduled end, and it has not been marked complete. The user explicitly chose the end time as the transition boundary.
- **Completed:** the occurrence has a saved completion record, which takes priority over the time-derived status.

The browser reads `Date.now()` every second with `setInterval`, and again on focus and visibility changes. It reads the actual device time each time; it does not increment a cached clock or wait until the next startup. Time-derived statuses are computed, not written as unfinished flags. Reopening after a break recalculates them immediately. Session times use the term timezone, including daylight-saving changes.

The UI has Overview, Courses & lectures, Catch up, and Term & settings. It includes week/term scope, previous/next week navigation, status filters and counts, search, next lecture, progress, course grouping/colours, course/session editors, completion/undo, and JSON backup export/restore. Upcoming occurrences do not count against progress. Example mode lives only in memory and does not overwrite the real planner. Removing a course removes its sessions and completion history after confirmation; editing term dates retains completion records.

## Course resources

Each course has an ID, name, numeric Scientia course number, Imperial Panopto folder UUID, and one of the supported colours (`sage`, `violet`, `blue`, `rose`, `amber`). Sessions reference the course instead of duplicating metadata. The folder input accepts a raw UUID or an Imperial Panopto folder URL. Legacy migrated courses may have blank resource fields until edited.

The user authorised assuming these university resource locations are static. Materials deliberately use the fixed `2627` path:

```text
https://scientia.doc.ic.ac.uk/2627/modules/<course-number>/materials
```

Recording links use the shared folder UUID and the specific occurrence date. The date is `MM/DD/YYYY`, with both startDate and endDate set to that date. Quoted values are URL-encoded in the fragment; this opens a date-filtered recording list rather than selecting a particular video.

The user supplied Quantum computing as the reference example:

```text
Course number: 70021
Folder UUID: 4394cd01-a3d6-489a-b98c-b49b011de184
Materials: https://scientia.doc.ic.ac.uk/2627/modules/70021/materials
Recording on 12 October 2026:
https://imperial.cloud.panopto.eu/Panopto/Pages/Sessions/List.aspx#folderID=%224394cd01-a3d6-489a-b98c-b49b011de184%22&startDate=%2210%2F12%2F2026%22&endDate=%2210%2F12%2F2026%22
```

University authentication and recording availability are handled by Scientia/Panopto. The local tracker does not retrieve or host those materials.

## Architecture and important files

Current package: `lecture-ledger-local`, version `1.0.0`, React 19, TypeScript, Vite 8, Tailwind CSS 4, and lucide-react icons. Backend: Node's HTTP server and built-in `node:sqlite` (`DatabaseSync`). Node.js **24.15 or newer** is required. No hosted authentication, Cloudflare runtime, or cloud database is needed by the current version.

| File | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `app/tracker.tsx` | Main UI, clock refresh, loading/saving planner, completion, term and backup controls |
| `app/course-components.tsx` | Course/session editors, shared links, tags |
| `app/globals.css` | App styling and responsive layout |
| `lib/model.ts` | Schema, validation, recurrence, timezone conversion, URL builders, legacy migration |
| `src/main.tsx`, `index.html` | React entry point and document shell |
| `server/index.mjs` | Loopback HTTP API, static frontend serving, Vite development middleware |
| `server/store.mjs` | SQLite storage, revisions, validation, backup parsing |
| `scripts/app.mjs`, `app.sh`, `open.sh`, `stop.sh` | Background/foreground lifecycle, private instance controls, graphical menu, desktop shortcut installation |
| `scripts/start.mjs`, `start.sh`, `scripts/node.sh`, `scripts/build.mjs` | Shared Node selection, initial dependency install/build, fingerprint-based rebuild and foreground startup |
| `server/config.mjs`, `ledger.config.example.json` | Persisted preferred port, strict validation, environment precedence |
| `scripts/transfer.mjs` | Command-line backup import/export |
| `scripts/test-model.mjs` | Recurrence, status, URLs, validation and migration checks |
| `scripts/test-local-server.mjs` | Isolated SQLite/API persistence and request checks |
| `scripts/test-app.mjs` | Isolated launcher, port conflict, shutdown, persistence, concurrency and shortcut/dialog checks |
| `README.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`, `.codex`, `AGENTS.md` | Setup, history, maintained context, coding instructions |

The v2 planner contains `schemaVersion: 2`, a nullable term, courses, weekly lectures, and a completion map. Weekdays are Monday=0 through Sunday=6. Occurrences are generated from this planner rather than stored as individual database rows.

SQLite stores one planner JSON record with a revision and update timestamp. Saves use compare-and-update revisions; stale tabs receive HTTP 409 instead of silently overwriting newer data. Existing v1 planners/backups upgrade while preserving lecture IDs and completion keys. Backup format is `{ format: 'lecture-ledger', version: 2, data: planner }`; restore accepts versions 1 and 2.

Endpoints: `GET /api/planner` returns data/revision, `PUT /api/planner` saves validated data/revision, and `GET /api/backup` downloads JSON. Managed production startup also enables `GET /api/control/status` and `POST /api/control/shutdown`; both require the current instance's random bearer token, which is stored only in private ignored runtime state. Cross-site control requests are rejected. Shutdown uses the authenticated instance rather than sending signals to a saved PID. The server binds only to `127.0.0.1`, checks allowed Host and write Origin, limits request bodies to 2 MB, and serves only the built frontend in production. Unknown API routes and attempts to access private files are rejected. There is one local planner, shared by browsers using the same server; this is not a multi-user account service.

## Running, storage, and backups

On this Linux computer, desktop and applications-menu shortcuts are installed at `/home/dz/Desktop/Lecture Ledger.desktop` and `/home/dz/.local/share/applications/lecture-ledger.desktop`. Double-click to start in the background and open the browser; shortcut actions provide Shut down, Configure port and App controls. Some desktops require Allow Launching once. `./app.sh` opens a small control menu using the installed `zenity`. Reinstall shortcuts with `./app.sh install-shortcut` after moving/cloning the repository. Shortcut files contain the current checkout path and are generated outside Git.

From the active repository:

```sh
./open.sh
./stop.sh
./app.sh status
./app.sh port 3001
```

`open.sh` starts a detached server, opens its actual address in the default browser, and reuses an existing server from this checkout. Closing the terminal or browser does not stop it; `stop.sh` gracefully closes it and releases the port. `start.sh` remains the foreground alternative; Ctrl+C or `stop.sh` stops it. Simultaneous starts are serialized with a launch lock; abandoned locks recover when their owner process is gone. Runtime state and background logs are in the ignored `.ledger/` folder, with owner-only permissions. Status checks authenticate the running instance, and stale state cannot stop unrelated applications. Startup installs dependencies if absent and rebuilds the frontend when its source fingerprint changes. First installation needs internet; normal runs with installed dependencies work offline, except opening external university links.

The shell controls share suitable Node selection through `scripts/node.sh`: Node on PATH or the bundled Node on this computer. Optional `LEDGER_NODE_BIN` selects an executable. The bundled runtime used during development was `/home/dz/.cache/codex-runtimes/codex-primary-runtime/dependencies/node/bin/node` (24.19.0); the system Node was 20.20.0, so plain `npm` commands may require a suitable Node first. On other systems with Node 24.15+, use `npm ci`, `npm run open`, `npm run stop`, `npm run status`, or `npm start` for the foreground server. Desktop integration is Linux-specific.

Defaults: preferred port 3000 and `<repository>/data/lecture-ledger.sqlite`. The desktop Configure port dialog or `./app.sh port 3001` saves the port in ignored `ledger.config.json` (see the tracked example). Whole-number ports 1–65535 are accepted. If the preferred port is occupied, foreground, background and development startup try the next 20 ports (capped at 65535). The preference remains unchanged; startup logs/status report the actual address. Changing the preference takes effect on next startup; reopening an existing server retains its current address. `PORT` takes precedence for one run and requires that exact port without fallback. `LEDGER_DB_PATH` selects a database (prefer an absolute filename); changing ports does not change the database. `npm run dev` provides live frontend updates and the API on the same port and reads the same port preference; stop it with Ctrl+C. Development is not managed by desktop controls. Use a separate database for development/QA that modifies data. Tests additionally use temporary `LEDGER_CONFIG_PATH` and `LEDGER_RUNTIME_DIR` overrides to isolate settings and runtime state.

The database survives closing the browser and restarting the server. SQLite uses WAL and may create sidecar files. The database file has owner-only permissions on the tested Linux system. Data, sidecars, dependencies, build output, environment files, local port settings, private runtime state/logs, and conventionally named backups are ignored by Git. Shutdown lets requests finish, with a five-second limit for lingering HTTP connections, before closing SQLite. A source clone does not include the user's timetable.

Export/restore in Term & settings or use:

```sh
npm run export -- /path/to/lecture-ledger-backup.json
npm run import -- /path/to/lecture-ledger-backup.json
```

Export refuses to overwrite a file; import refuses to replace a nonempty timetable without `--replace`. Back up before replacing data. Stop the app before copying the raw database and any remaining sidecars. Never reset or commit the real database for testing.

